# Generated data files: never let Git attempt a textual merge on these.
#
# Everything under data/*.json is produced by a script (publish_cpurnsa.py,
# run_all_logs_refresh.py, publish_softs_diagnostics.py, etc.) from source
# CSVs/Parquet, not written by hand. A line-based git merge/rebase on two
# divergent versions of one of these files can leave content that is
# individually well-formed but structurally wrong - e.g. duplicated fields
# inside one JSON object, which happened on 2026-09-22 and broke
# cpurnsa.html in production.
#
# Two custom merge drivers handle these files. NEITHER ever interleaves lines
# from the two versions - a whole side is always chosen - so the structural
# corruption above cannot happen.
#
#   merge=newest  (tools/merge_newest_json.py)
#     The daily case. Two writers (this box's cron/manual pushes and the
#     github-actions bot) publish the same file to main, so it diverges most
#     days. These files carry a top-level "generated_at_utc"; the driver keeps
#     whichever side's timestamp is later ("last wins"), which is exactly what
#     the manual rebase resolution did by hand. It falls back to a normal
#     stop-and-ask conflict (never a silent or partial merge) whenever it
#     cannot decide: either side fails to parse, lacks "generated_at_utc", or
#     both sides share a timestamp but differ in content.
#     Register once per clone (not committed, lives in .git/config):
#       git config merge.newest.driver \
#         "python3 tools/merge_newest_json.py %O %A %B %P"
#     If the driver is NOT registered, Git treats merge=newest as an unknown
#     driver and falls back to the built-in text merge - which is unsafe for
#     these files - so registration is required on any clone that does merges.
#
#   merge=binary
#     For files with no reliable timestamp to compare (currently only
#     jup_gear_chart.json). Always reports a conflict instead of attempting a
#     line-based merge, the same way Git treats binary files. To resolve: do
#     NOT hand-edit conflict markers; re-run the file's generator against the
#     current inputs and `git add` the fresh output, or pick a whole side.
#
# Note on merge=ours (deliberately unused): it resolves conflicts silently by
# keeping the local side and discarding the incoming side, which for files
# updated by automated cron/CI jobs could silently drop real pipeline data.
data/cpurnsa_curve_history.json merge=newest
data/cpurnsa_daily_commentary.json merge=newest
data/cpurnsa_pca_diagnostics.json merge=newest
data/health.json merge=newest
data/uscpi_release_manifest.json merge=newest
data/softs_diagnostics.json merge=newest
data/bars_etf_logs.json merge=newest
data/equity_vol_logs.json merge=newest
data/jup_gear_chart.json merge=binary
data/jup_model_logs.json merge=newest
data/jup_performance_fee.json merge=newest
data/refresh_status.json merge=newest
data/swaption_status.json merge=newest
data/tips_fedinvest_status.json merge=newest
